{"name":"Sugar Creek","hex":"#c96fa8","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Sugar Creek","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A watercourse:\nA creek, a tributary of the Driftwood River, in Indiana.\nA different creek, a tributary of the Wabash River, in Indiana.\nA stream in Tennessee, a tributary of Duck River.\nA stream in Minnesota.\nA tributary of the Tuscarawas River, Ohio.\nA tributary of the Susquehanna River, Pennsylvania.","A creek, a tributary of the Driftwood River, in Indiana."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Sugaw Creek (“creek in North Carolina and settlements named after it”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Sugar_Creek"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sugar Creek Township, Shelby County, Indiana","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar Creek Township is one of fourteen townships in Shelby County, Indiana. As of the 2010 census, its population was 1,086 and it contained 412 housing units."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Map_highlighting_Sugar_Creek_Township%2C_Shelby_County%2C_Indiana.svg/330px-Map_highlighting_Sugar_Creek_Township%2C_Shelby_County%2C_Indiana.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar_Creek_Township%2C_Shelby_County%2C_Indiana"},{"title":"Sugar Creek, Missouri","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar Creek is a city in both Jackson and Clay counties in Missouri, United States. The population was 3,271 at the 2020 census. It is part of the Kansas City metropolitan area."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Sugar_Creek_City_Hall.jpg/330px-Sugar_Creek_City_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar_Creek%2C_Missouri"},{"title":"Sugar","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar is a class of sweet-tasting, soluble carbohydrates, many of which are used in food. Simple sugars, also called monosaccharides, include glucose, fructose and galactose. Compound sugars, also called disaccharides or double sugars, are molecules made of two bonded monosaccharides; common examples are sucrose, lactose and maltose. White sugar is almost pure sucrose. During digestion, compound sugars are hydrolysed into simple sugars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg/330px-Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Jesse_James_Cave_Near_Sugar_Creek_Missouri.jpg/500px-Jesse_James_Cave_Near_Sugar_Creek_Missouri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Jesse_James_Cave_Near_Sugar_Creek_Missouri.jpg/500px-Jesse_James_Cave_Near_Sugar_Creek_Missouri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kids exploring the Jesse James Cave, high on the bluffs overlooking the Missouri River near Sugar Creek, Missouri in 1950.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJesse_James_Cave_Near_Sugar_Creek_Missouri.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Liberty-bend-bridge.jpg/500px-Liberty-bend-bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Liberty-bend-bridge.jpg/1280px-Liberty-bend-bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Liberty-bend-bridge.jpg/500px-Liberty-bend-bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Liberty Bend Bridge, built in 1949 to replace the old Liberty Bend Bridge two miles north.","link":"https://en.wikipe